INT. MUSEUM OF MINIATURE HOUSES - NIGHT

A hush of glass cases. Dollhouses on pedestals: Victorian mansions, adobe cottages, a tiny lighthouse. Each lit from within by pin-sized lamps.

PRUDENCE KELLAWAY (40s, curator's blazer, flashlight clipped to her belt) makes her rounds, brushing dust from a case with a feather duster. Behind her, trailing a step too close, comes ZINNIA BELLWETHER (20s, intern, overlarge cardigan, tote bag of cleaning supplies).

                    PRUDENCE
          Mind the north wing. The Whitcombe house is on a loose pedestal.

                    ZINNIA
          Which one is Whitcombe?

                    PRUDENCE
          The one with the attic.

Zinnia peers at a case. A Georgian townhouse, its windows aglow. In the tiny attic window, a doll sits in a rocking chair, facing the glass.

                    ZINNIA
          She's looking at me.

                    PRUDENCE
          They're all looking at someone.

She keeps walking. Zinnia lingers. She bends close. Her breath fogs the glass.

                    ZINNIA
          Was she facing the wall earlier?

                    PRUDENCE
          Who?

                    ZINNIA
          The doll. This afternoon. She was facing the wall.

                    PRUDENCE
          Dolls don't move.

                    ZINNIA
          I know.

Prudence turns, annoyed, to check. She stops. Her face goes still. She crouches beside Zinnia, both women at the level of the miniature window.

In the attic, the little rocking chair is rocking. Slowly. Back, forth. The doll's head has turned toward them.

Prudence grips Zinnia's arm. Zinnia grips back. Neither speaks.

The tiny lamps in the Georgian house flicker out, one floor at a time, until only the attic glows.

                    PRUDENCE
          (barely a whisper)
          Back away.

                    ZINNIA
          I can't.

                    PRUDENCE
          Why not?

                    ZINNIA
          She's holding my hand.

Prudence looks down. Zinnia's fingers, still wrapped around her sleeve, are pale. A thread of white yarn runs from Zinnia's cuff, taut, across the floor, and into a hairline gap beneath the case.

Prudence snaps the thread with a tug. The doll in the attic tips from her chair and tumbles out of sight. The lamp dies. The whole museum goes dark except for the flashlight at Prudence's belt, trembling with her breath.

                    PRUDENCE (CONT'D)
          We're leaving.

                    ZINNIA
          Yes.

They run. Behind them, in a hundred miniature houses, a hundred tiny lights wink on.
